|~ What is declared under `::set`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of sets, each with the parts of its args and its answer.
   A set is the vector in the one order
   without duplicates: a verb that keeps its subject's kind answers a
   set through the constructor of `::set`, and one that imposes an order
   answers the vector. ~|

:set/construct
  |~~ The set of the elements of a vector, in the one order without duplicates. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::set` builds a set from a vector, `::set[3 1 3]`; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:set/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::set{:not :a-vec} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::set/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:set/count
  |~~ The number of elements. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/count/countsTheElements
  |~~ Two elements count two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | count | eq 2)

:set/count/size
  |~~ How many elements the set holds. ~~|
  ::number

:set/empty
  |~~ Whether the set holds no element. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/empty/holdsOfTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set is empty. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | empty | eq true)

:set/empty/vacant
  |~~ Whether the set holds no element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/first
  |~~ The least element, or null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/first/answersTheLeast
  |~~ The first element is the least. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:c :a :b] | first | eq :a)

:set/last
  |~~ The greatest element, or null for the empty set. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/last/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ The last element is the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:c :a :b] | last | eq :c)

:set/sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements, each a number. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sum/addsTheElements
  |~~ The sum adds every element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | sum | eq 6)

:set/sum/total
  |~~ The sum of the elements. ~~|
  ::number

:set/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element in the one
      order that is none.
  ~~|

:set/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "two"] | sum !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::set/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:set/avg
  |~~ The mean of the elements, each a number, or null when there is none. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one, two and six is three. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 6] | avg | eq 3)

:set/avg/mean
  |~~ The mean of the elements, null for an empty set. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:set/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of numbers; `:index` names the first element
      in the one order that is none.
  ~~|

:set/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the numbers is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 "two"] | avg !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::set/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string 1])

:set/min
  |~~ The least element, or the one whose key answers the least value. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/min/answersTheLeast
  |~~ Without a key the least element answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | min | eq 1)

:set/min/readsTheKey
  |~~ A descending key turns the least into the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | min ~(desc) | eq 4)

:set/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/max
  |~~ The greatest element, or the one whose key answers the greatest
      value.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/max/answersTheGreatest
  |~~ Without a key the greatest element answers. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | max | eq 4)

:set/max/readsTheKey
  |~~ A descending key turns the greatest into the least. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1 4] | max ~(desc) | eq 1)

:set/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/filter
  |~~ The set of the elements whose predicate answers true. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/filter/keepsWhatThePredicateHolds
  |~~ The elements past two are kept. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq #[3 4 5])

:set/filter/dropsTheRest
  |~~ The elements the predicate rejects go. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 -2 3] | filter ~(gt 0) | eq #[1 3])

:set/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/filter/kept
  |~~ The elements the predicate accepted. ~~|
  ::set

:set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | filter ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::set/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:set/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/every/holdsOfEveryElement
  |~~ Every element of the set is positive. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 4] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:set/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/every/all
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for every element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | every ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::set/every/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:set/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/any/holdsOfSomeElement
  |~~ Some element is past two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)

:set/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/any/some
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for some element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at the element. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | any ~(1) !| [type /index /actualType] | eq [::set/any/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])

:set/groupBy
  |~~ The map of sets the key sorts the elements into. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/groupBy/filesIntoSets
  |~~ The elements file into sets under the key they answer. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(if (gt 1) ~(:big) ~(:small)) | /big | eq #[2 3])

:set/groupBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/groupBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/groupBy/groups
  |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered, each bucket a set. ~~|
  ::map

:set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text; any other value is
      refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesABooleanKey
  |~~ An element that is a boolean files under no key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[true] | groupBy !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::set/groupBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::boolean 0])

:set/indexBy
  |~~ The map of the elements under the key each answers, or under
      itself without a key.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/indexBy/filesEachUnderItsKey
  |~~ Each element files under its key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[{:id :a}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a | eq {:id :a})

:set/indexBy/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/indexBy/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/indexBy/index
  |~~ Each element under the keyword its key answered, the last of alike ones. ~~|
  ::map

:set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text; any other value is
      refused, and `:index` names the element.
  ~~|

:set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ An element that is a vector files under no key. ~~|
  ::law~(#[[1]] | indexBy !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::set/indexBy/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:set/sort
  |~~ The vector of the elements in the one order, or by the value the
      key answers for each; the order is imposed, so the answer is the
      vector.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sort/answersAVector
  |~~ The sorted elements are a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:y :x :z] | sort | eq [:x :y :z])

:set/sort/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:set/sort/sorted
  |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them, a vector. ~~|
  ::vec

:set/take
  |~~ The set of the least count elements. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/take/takesTheLeast
  |~~ Two of a set are its least two. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2 | eq #[:a :b])

:set/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/take/taken
  |~~ The least elements. ~~|
  ::set

:set/drop
  |~~ The set of the elements after the least count. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/drop/dropsTheLeast
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the greatest. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2 | eq #[:c :d])

:set/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/drop/rest
  |~~ The elements after the least ones dropped. ~~|
  ::set

:set/at
  |~~ The element at the index in the one order, a negative one
      counting from the end, or null past either end.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/at/countsFromTheEnd
  |~~ A negative index counts from the end. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | at -1 | eq :c)

:set/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:set/at/index
  |~~ The index, a negative one counting from the end. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/at/index/NotNumberError
  |~~ The index is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:set/at/index/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The index is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:set/distinct
  |~~ The set itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/distinct/isTheSetItself
  |~~ A set is distinct already. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/distinct/unique
  |~~ The set itself. ~~|
  ::set

:set/reverse
  |~~ The vector of the elements in the reverse of the one order. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reverse/answersAVector
  |~~ The reversed elements are a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | reverse | eq [:c :b :a])

:set/reverse/reversed
  |~~ The elements from the greatest down, a vector. ~~|
  ::vec

:set/flat
  |~~ The set of the elements with one level of nesting spliced in, so
      a set of sets flattens into their union.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/flat/unitesTheSetsItHolds
  |~~ A set of sets flattens into their union. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat | eq #[1 2 3])

:set/flat/flattened
  |~~ The elements with the sets among them spliced in. ~~|
  ::set

:set/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the elements in the one order by the reducer. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reduce/foldsInTheOneOrder
  |~~ The fold adds the elements to the seed. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)

:set/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:set/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:set/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one verb, which the fold calls with the
      accumulator as its subject and the element as its first arg,
      `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a
      name of no verb is refused.
  ~~|

:set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesAPipeline
  |~~ A reducer of two steps is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::set/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:set/union
  |~~ The set of the elements of either set, merged in the one order;
      left without the other set, the union of the sets the set holds.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/union/mergesTwoSets
  |~~ The union holds the elements of either. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c] | eq #[:a :b :c])

:set/union/unitesTheSetsItHolds
  |~~ Without the other set, the union of the sets it holds. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[1] #[2]] | union | eq #[1 2])

:set/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/union/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/union/merged
  |~~ The elements of both sets. ~~|
  ::set

:set/union/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `union` over a set its call leaves without the other operand joins
      the operands the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/union/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | union !| type | eq ::set/union/NoOperandsError)

:set/union/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `union` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/union/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | union !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::set/union/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:set/union/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/union/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/minus
  |~~ The set of the elements the other set lacks. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/minus/removesTheOtherElements
  |~~ The elements of the other set go. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b] | eq #[:a :c])

:set/minus/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/minus/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/minus/remaining
  |~~ The elements the other set does not hold. ~~|
  ::set

:set/minus/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `minus` over a set its call leaves without the other operand joins
      the operands the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/minus/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | minus !| type | eq ::set/minus/NoOperandsError)

:set/minus/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `minus` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::set/minus/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:set/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/minus/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/inter
  |~~ The set of the elements both sets hold. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/inter/keepsTheCommonElements
  |~~ The elements both hold stay. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d] | eq #[:b])

:set/inter/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/inter/with/NotSetOrNullError
  |~~ The with is a set or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::set ::null)

:set/inter/common
  |~~ The elements both sets hold. ~~|
  ::set

:set/inter/NoOperandsError
  |~~ `inter` over a set its call leaves without the other operand joins
      the operands the set holds, so the set holds one at least.
  ~~|

:set/inter/NoOperandsError/refusesTheEmptySet
  |~~ The empty set holds no operand. ~~|
  ::law~(#[] | inter !| type | eq ::set/inter/NoOperandsError)

:set/inter/OperandsMismatchedError
  |~~ The operands `inter` joins inside a set are two sets or two maps. ~~|

:set/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/refusesASetAndAMap
  |~~ A set and a map do not join. ~~|
  ::law~(#[#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter !| [type /leftType /rightType] | eq [::set/inter/OperandsMismatchedError ::set ::map])

:set/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/inter/OperandsMismatchedError/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the operand on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:set/has
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element, found by a binary search in
      the one order.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/has/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | has :b | eq true)

:set/has/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/has/held
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean

:set/contains
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element, the membership `has` asks,
      under the word a string answers for its substrings.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/contains/findsAnElement
  |~~ An element held is found. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | contains :b | eq true)

:set/contains/element
  |~~ The element the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:set/contains/found
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element. ~~|
  ::boolean
